// SR 143.1 — Section 1 General Provisions
// generated by src/parse.py. Do not edit by hand.

:NOTE
English is not an official language of the Swiss Confederation. This translation is provided for information purposes only and has no legal force.

:INFO
Art. 1 Identity documents
1 All Swiss nationals are entitled to one of each type of identity document.
2 Identity documents within the meaning of this Act serve as proof of Swiss nationality and of the identity for the holder.
3 The Federal Council shall determine the types of identity document and regulate the special features of identity documents whose holders enjoy privileges and immunities under the Vienna Convention of 18 April 1961 on Diplomatic Relations or the Vienna Convention of 24 April 1963 on Consular Relations.
|

:NOTE
Para. 3 — SR 0.191.01
Para. 3 — SR 0.191.02
|

:INFO
Art. 2 Content of the document
1 Each identity document must contain the following data:
a. official surname;
b. first names;
c. sex;
d. date of birth;
e. place of origin;
f. nationality;
g. height;
h. signature;
i. photograph;
j. issuing authority;
k. date of issue;
l. date of expiry of validity;
m. ID document number and type.
2 The data specified in letters a-d, f, and k-m are also contained on the identity document in machine-readable form.
2bis The identity document may be provided with a data chip. The data chip may contain a facial image and the fingerprints of the holder. The other identity document data referred to in paragraphs 1, 3, 4 and 5 may also be stored on the chip.
2ter The Federal Council shall determine which types of identity documents are provided with a chip and which data are to be stored on it. It shall ensure that an identity document without a chip may also be applied for.
|

:INFO
Art. 2 (continued)
2quater The identity document may also contain electronic identities for authentication, signature and encryption functions.
3 The identity document may contain restrictions on the extent of validity.
4 At the request of the applicant, the identity document may contain compound, religious, stage or partnership names, and details of special identifiers such as disabilities, prostheses or implants.
5 On request, the identity document for minors may contain the names of the minor’s legal representative.
|

:NOTE
Para. 2bis — Inserted by Art. 2 No 1 of the Federal Act of 13 June 2008 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EC on Biometric Passports and Travel Documents, in force since 1 March 2010 (AS 2009 5521; BBl 2007 5159).
Para. 2ter — Inserted by Art. 2 No 1 of the Federal Act of 13 June 2008 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EC on Biometric Passports and Travel Documents, in force since 1 March 2010 (AS 2009 5521; BBl 2007 5159).
Para. 2ter — Second sentence inserted by No I of the Federal Act of 17 June 2011 (Obtaining Non-Biometric Identity Cards from the Commune of Residence), in force since 1 March 2012. (AS 2011 5003; BBl 2011 2277 2291).
|

:NOTE
Para. 2quater — Inserted by Art. 2 No 1 of the Federal Act of 13 June 2008 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EC on Biometric Passports and Travel Documents, in force since 1 March 2010 (AS 2009 5521; BBl 2007 5159).
Para. 4 — Amended by Art. 2 No 1 of the Federal Act of 13 June 2008 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EC on Biometric Passports and Travel Documents, in force since 1 March 2010 (AS 2009 5521; BBl 2007 5159).
Para. 5 — Term in accordance with Annex No 2 of the Federal Act of 19 Dec. 2008 (Adult Protection, Law on Persons, Law on Children), in force since 1 Jan. 2013 (AS 2011 725; BBl 2006 7001). This amendment has been made throughout the text.
|

:NOTE
Para. 5 — Term in accordance with Annex No 2 of the Federal Act of 19 Dec. 2008 (Adult Protection, Law on Persons, Law on Children), in force since 1 Jan. 2013 (AS 2011 725; BBl 2006 7001). This amendment has been made throughout the text.
|

:INFO
Art. 2a Security and reading of the data chip
1 The data chip shall be protected against forgery and unauthorised reading. The Federal Council shall determine the corresponding technical requirements.
2 The Federal Council is authorised to conclude agreements with other states on the reading of the fingerprints stored in the chip, provided that the states concerned have a level of data protection that is equivalent to that in Switzerland.
3 It may authorise transport companies, airport operators and other appropriate bodies that need to check a person's identity to read the fingerprints stored on the chip.
|

:NOTE
Art. 2a — Inserted by Art. 2 No 1 of the Federal Act of 13 June 2008 on the Approval and Implementation of the Exchange of Notes between Switzerland and the EC on Biometric Passports and Travel Documents, in force since 1 March 2010 (AS 2009 5521; BBl 2007 5159).
|

:INFO
Art. 3 Validity period
Identity documents are valid for a limited period of time. The Federal Council shall regulate their period of validity.

